----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, P: d7-d6, Q: d8-e7, Q: d8-f6, Q: d8-g5, Q: d8-h4, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, B: f8-e7, B: f8-d6, B: f8-c5, B: f8-b4, B: f8-a3, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation: Looking at this position, White has aggressively placed two knights on d5 and e5 in the center of the board. This is an unusual opening setup. Let me evaluate key candidate moves:\n\n1. **Q: d8-h4** - Queen to h4 puts immediate pressure on White's position, attacking f2 (a critical weakness near White's king) and potentially forcing defensive moves.\n\n2. **B: f8-b4** - Bishop to b4 develops a piece with a check threat, putting pressure on White's position and developing naturally.\n\n3. **N: b8-c6** - Develops the knight and attacks White's centralized knight on e5, challenging White's strong center.\n\n4. **P: d7-d6** - Attacks the knight on e5, forcing it to move, but this is somewhat passive and blocks my dark-squared bishop's development.\n\n5. **B: f8-c5** - Develops the bishop actively to a strong diagonal, aiming at f2, another critical square near White's king.\n\nWhite's setup with both knights forward is aggressive but also leaves weaknesses. The f2 square is particularly vulnerable as it's only defended by the king. Q: d8-h4 puts maximum immediate pressure on this weakness and forces White to respond to the threat of Qxf2+, which would be devastating.\n\nReason: Queen to h4 is the most forcing move, creating an immediate tactical threat against f2 (Qxf2+ would be checkmate if the king is on e1!). This move develops the queen aggressively, exploits White's premature knight advances, and puts White on the defensive immediately. It's a principled aggressive response to White's unusual opening play.\n\nAnswer: Q: d8-h4"}
